{"actor_ref":"claude","class":"draft_history","document_uuid":"01a1a54a-31f5-70e2-912b-c3988e1a9d77","id":"01a1a665-380d-7466-9b59-5b342f079d05","idempotency_key":"6f76b5a35678ba1024065433028401edd273a6bb1ba4abf483bbc6d5ab7f174b","occurred_at":"2026-11-04T11:50:13Z","payload":{"note":"Closed 5/5. A checkbox per chapter writes progress for the current user and a 1/N counter in the study header follows it - verified in the browser as 1/3, with the box still checked after re-opening the study. The chapter you last read is remembered per study in localStorage rather than the database, because it is a personal cursor and not something another reader of the same study should see. attempts/score/hints/mastery are recorded now and unused by the UI: a schema that does not record them cannot be retrofitted without a migration, so a test asserts they survive rather than trusting a column to be added later. The item that mattered most is i-2d03: a test proves ticking a chapter changes neither the study's updated timestamp, nor its version counter, nor the published bundle bytes, and that the bundle contains no completion data at all. Reading a study must not alter it, and that is now a check rather than an intention. Progress isolation has its own test file: per user (alex completed, sam has not, with his own attempt count), per study, and a clean empty answer for a user who never opened it.","target":"t-bb94"},"type":"work.note_added","v":2}
